  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 PM EDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nThe cold front is now sitting over central Kentucky as it continues \nto the southeast. Winds are fairly light ahead of the front with 5 \nto 10 mph winds generally out of the north behind it. This is \ndropping temperatures into the mid to upper 60s across southern \nIndiana and near the Ohio River in central Kentucky. The earlier low \nlevel jet has moved out of the area to the northeast as 2 inch peak \nprecipitable water values along the front are beginning to drop off \nand retreat to the south. This trend is expected to continue through \nthe evening, reducing rainfall rates over central Kentucky. \n\nTonight, the heaviest rain will stay closer to the higher moisture, \nprecipitable water values in the 1.8 to 2 inch range, and slide east \nacross southern Kentucky. Areas farther to the north will see \nlighter more scattered showers. Most of the rain will move off to \nthe east by sunrise Saturday morning with lows in the low 50s in \nsouthern Indiana to low to mid 60s in far southern Kentucky.\n\nThrough the day Saturday, a few isolated showers are expected to \ndrift northeast through the Lake Cumberland area, but all other \nareas in the CWA are expected to remain dry with mostly sunny skies \nexpected over the northern two thirds of the CWA. Surface high \npressure centered over the Great Lakes will keep east-northeast \nwinds in place as most places see the low to mid 70s for highs. \nThere will likely be a few places that only see the upper 60s \ntowards eastern Kentucky. \n\nSaturday night, clear skies, light winds, and moisture from today \nwill likely lead to patchy fog as radiative cooling drops \ntemperatures towards dew points. \n\nSunday, stubborn cloud cover is expected to remain along and south \nof the parkways during the day, but as we head into the overnight \nperiod, a dry cold front will get pushed southeast towards the Lower \nOhio Valley by northwest upper flow. This will clear skies and bring \nanother round of light CAA ahead of another surface high behind the \nfront, keeping highs in the upper 60s to mid 70s for Monday and \nTuesday. Monday and Tuesday night, some low temperatures could drop \ninto the upper 30s to low 40s before the high pushes east through \nthe Great Lakes, returning WAA. Highs get lifted into the upper 70s \nto low 80s on Wednesday before inching slightly warmer for the end \nof the workweek.",
